WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Outside London's pay-as-you-go area, trains are National Rail. There you do need a ticket: from the ticket office, the machine, the app or the website. Single: one way. Return: there and back.

2
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:29.700
Off-peak: cheaper, outside rush hours. Advance: usually valid only on one specific train. Buy your ticket before you board. If there is no way to buy one, keep proof.

3
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:45.700
If you travel without a valid ticket you can be given a «penalty fare». On National Rail in England it is £100 plus the ticket price, or £50 plus the ticket if you pay within 21 days (in force since January 2023).

4
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:57.900
If you think it is unfair, you can appeal within the deadline on the notice. TfL has its own fine: check the current amount on its website.

5
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:13.900
A Railcard gives you 1/3 off many National Rail tickets. There are several: the 16-25 Railcard, the 26-30 Railcard (digital only) and the Network Railcard (London and the south-east), among others. They have a price and conditions: check them on railcard.co.uk before you buy one.

6
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:21.100
Now do the lesson exercises. If you are unsure, watch this video again.
